    "what": "The port-type law of the flow graph: a wire may only carry a value the target port accepts.",
    "law": "A drag from `extrusion-axis@vectorOut` (a math.vector output) released on `extrude@wire` (a brep.solid.extrude wire input) was ACCEPTED: the graph disconnected the valid `profile@wire->extrude@wire` and re-solved the model with a vector where a geometry handle belongs. `board::is_valid_connection` checked handle role, self-node, duplicate edge and acyclicity, never the port's declared type, and `IoPortSpec.value_type` carried the OPERATOR-ID list a channel was declared under, not a value type, so no surface could tell the pair apart.",
    "rule": "Every channel declares the VALUE SCHEMAS it carries — the `Dictionary::schema()` a value on that wire actually has. An output lists what it may produce, an input every schema it accepts. A pair is refused when both sides declare and the declared sets are DISJOINT; an undeclared channel stays connectable, so no shipped graph is retro-refused. The declarations come from the contributions themselves (`ChannelSpec::with_value_types` in the registered operators and the schema-component derivation), never from a table keyed by operator id.",
